<p>
Rachel Yang is a studying to be a research scientist. She&rsquo;s currently a Ph.D. student at MIT but has already worked on some projects leading to breakthroughs. She tells the story of the first such project when she was an undergraduate.
</p>
<p>
The project itself was a success. After some trial and error she managed to produce the desired result. That&rsquo;s when the problem began. Her professor asked her to write up her results as a research paper. She panicked. She was perfectly confident while doing her research but  was convinced that she couldn&rsquo;t write intelligibly.
</p>
<p>
What she discovered was that <a href="https://www.science.org/content/article/i-hated-writing-until-i-learned-there-s-science-it">writing was a lot like doing science</a>. You could think of it as trial and error. You produce a first draft and fix the obvious problems with it. You iterate on that process until you reach a satisfactory product. Just like doing science.
</p>
<p>
This seems to me to be an appropriate story for Irreal because many in our industry hate writing and feel they aren&rsquo;t good at it. As Yang explains, it just a matter of mindset. Writing is not unlike what you do everyday in your job: make a first stab, improve on that, iterate.
</p>
